#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""tools/8f-movetype-gate -- the LIVE gate for plan_entity.md step 8f-movetypes.

    tools/8f-movetype-gate --self-test     the reasoning, with no game
    tools/8f-movetype-gate                 the live run
    tools/8f-movetype-gate --keep          leave the game up afterwards

NEEDS tools/8f-probe-movetypes APPLIED and the tree rebuilt. Read that file
first: MOVETYPE_OBSERVER, MOVETYPE_FLY and MOVETYPE_FLYGRAVITY are unreachable
in this build (measured at step 8f-movetypes across all 391 $File rows of the
two VPC scripts that then defined the build -- now the source lists in
src/{server,client}/native/CMakeLists.txt -- no compiled TU wrote any of the
three onto a player), so a gate that drove a player normally could not reach
the code it is gating.

=============================================================================
WHY EVERY CHECK HERE IS A PAIR, AND NEVER "IT MOVED"
=============================================================================
The named trap for this step is that observer movement is the one a gate will
not notice: a player who cannot move as an observer looks exactly like a player
standing still. "It did not crash" and "the origin changed" both pass on a build
where all three arms do nothing -- the second because an arm that does nothing
leaves a player who was already falling to keep falling.

So no arm is judged on one reading. Each is a PAIR of readings taken at the same
place, under the same command, differing only in the thing being gated:

  fly / flygravity   The SAME move type family in the air, with and without the
                     one line that separates them. MOVETYPE_FLY hangs (no
                     gravity) and MOVETYPE_FLYGRAVITY falls. An arm that did
                     nothing would hang in BOTH -- so `flygravity falls` kills
                     the do-nothing build, and `fly hangs` kills a build that
                     silently fell through to MOVETYPE_WALK's body, which also
                     falls.

  fly coast          MOVETYPE_FLY applies no friction at all -- FullTossMove
                     never calls Friction(). So a flier that is let go KEEPS
                     MOVING, where a walker stops within a fraction of a second.
                     This is a signature no other arm in the loop has.

  observer roam /    The SAME move type, the SAME place, the SAME +forward, and
  observer fixed     only the OBSERVER MODE different: roaming moves, fixed does
                     not. Nothing but FullObserverMove's own switch can produce
                     that difference, which is what makes this pair evidence
                     about the ported code rather than about the player.

  managed loop       And the movement counter must GROW in every arm above.
                     Otherwise the readings are about movement, not about
                     MANAGED movement -- the check that cannot fail, in the form
                     this project has shipped it eight times.

  ARM 7 WAS THE OTHER HALF OF THAT AND IT WAS RETIRED AT STEP 8f-remove. It set
  sourcesharp_movement_managed 0 and required the counter to go FLAT, which said
  the growth above was the switch and not a free-running counter. On this build
  that command is an Error() that does not return -- game/shared/gamemovement.cpp
  left both builds (then the two vpcs; neither CMake target compiles it), so
  there is nothing for the 0 arm to select. The arm then
  PASSED FOR THE WRONG REASON, which is worse than failing: the counter is read
  out of the log, the process was dead, so the last logged value answered and
  "flat" meant "nobody is running". Measured on this tree --

      == 7. control: sourcesharp_movement_managed 0
             counter 2989 -> 2989 with the port off
        ok    and it is FLAT (2989 -> 2989)
      == GATE PASSED (21 check(s))

  and console.log carries the FATAL, printed by the same command, four lines
  after the `sourcesharp_movement_managed 0` the gate had just sent.

  WHAT REPLACED IT is arm F below: the same command, required to PRINT its
  refusal and STOP THE PROCESS. What is lost is the free-running-counter
  control, and the honest replacement for that is not a counter at all -- it is
  arm 6, where the loop RAN (the counter grew) and the player did not move.
  Every arm here samples DISPLACEMENT at the origin as well as the counter, and
  the displacement is what each pass hangs on.
=============================================================================
"""
